(Discrepancy 2 — a LIST in a format string used as a VALUE renders the
 list's raw pointer instead of the list. LANGUAGE.md:3130-3131 says a
 format string used as a value "materializes into a fresh NUL-terminated
 string"; 3158-3161 says one shared name resolver makes every sink render
 identically. The same slot in a Print renders correctly, so the two
 sinks disagree. Every value context is affected — initializer,
 assignment, and comparison operand alike.
 Lines 1 and 2 below are the list printed directly and through a print
 slot: both correct. Line 3 is the same slot held in a text first, and it
 is written as a comparison rather than a raw print because the address
 varies per run and this probe has to re-run clean.
 Second facet, not checkable in a fixed expected block for the same
 reason: `Print "{arguments's all}".` renders a pointer in the PRINT sink
 too, where an ordinary list in a print slot is correct. Run on its own:
   Print "{arguments's all}".        ->  140642937016320
   Print arguments's all.            ->  []
 The buffer sink cannot be probed for either facet: it segfaults on the
 argument text properties (Discrepancy 1).
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox probe.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   [1, 2, 3]
   [1, 2, 3]
   a list in a format string held as a value is NOT the list form
)
a list called ordinary is [1, 2, 3].
a text called 'the held list' is "{ordinary}".
Print ordinary.
Print "{ordinary}".
If 'the held list' is equal to "[1, 2, 3]" then, Print "a list in a format string held as a value is the list form". Otherwise, Print "a list in a format string held as a value is NOT the list form".
